The simplest example demonstrating RVO/NRVO is returning a locally constructed object:

```c++
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass(int val) : value(val) {}
    int value;
};

MyClass createObject() {
    MyClass obj(10); // Object created on the stack within the function
    return obj;      // RVO/NRVO may eliminate the copy/move here
}

int main() {
    MyClass myObj = createObject();
    std::cout << myObj.value << std::endl; // Output: 10
}
```

Note:  The compiler is *not* obligated to perform RVO/NRVO.  Certain circumstances, such as returning an object constructed from a complex expression or the presence of exceptions, might prevent it.  However, modern compilers are quite sophisticated in their optimization capabilities.
